NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON PENNOYER V. NEFF
1. One might perceive Pennoyer v. Neff as a proto-Mullane: in the earlier litigation brought by Mitchell against Neff, notice by publication in the local Multnomah County, Oregon newspaper, with no other means attempted, does not satisfy due process. Given that the plaintiff Mitchell knew the defendant Neff was out of state (he was in California), it was obvious publication would be ineffective. Compare Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Tr. Co., 339 U.S. 306, 315 (1950) (“It would be idle to pretend that publication alone, as prescribed here, is a reliable means of acquainting interested parties of the fact that their rights are before the courts.”). And given that Mitchell could have, but did not, seek a judicial attachment of Neff’s property in Oregon, there were superior means of attempting to obtain actual notice that were not employed. Compare Mullane, 339 U.S. at 316 (“When the state within which the owner has located such property seizes it for some reason, publication or posting affords an additional measure of notification.”). As such, this case is exceptional only insofar as it rejected the willingness of many states at that time to permit the exercise of judicial authority in circumstances involving only the barest forms of constructive notice.
